meta {
  name: cancel-my-order
  type: http
  seq: 8
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{upi_order_uid}}/cancel
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Guest-Token: {{guest_token}}
}

tests {
  test("cancels a placed order", function () {
    // Strict 200, not "200 or 409". This targets the UPI order, which nothing else in the
    // collection touches, so it is always still `placed` when this runs. Accepting a 409 here
    // would let a real regression pass.
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("the order is now cancelled", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.status).to.equal("cancelled")
  })

  test("and the cancel button is gone", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.can_guest_cancel).to.equal(false)
  })
}

docs {
  # Cancel (diner)

Allowed **only while the order is `placed`** (docs/DECISIONS.md D6). Once staff accepts, the kitchen
may have started, and this answers `TX_ORD_010` (409).

That 409 is a race with a definite outcome, not an error: the correct client behaviour is to refetch
and let the button disappear, not to alarm the diner.

This targets the **UPI order** from `place-order-upi`, deliberately not `order_uid` -- that one is
left alone so `admin-orders` can drive it through the full lifecycle.

To see the 409 instead, point it at `{{order_uid}}` and run it after `admin-orders / accept-order`.
}
